## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Why It Breaks

| Removed API | .NET 8 Behavior |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` Can Be Made Cancellation-Aware (Recommended)

For **reliable** cancellation, the work itself must cooperate:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                       .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
                       .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}

// Caller example — work checks the token at regular intervals:
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in largeCollection)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();  // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return "done";
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---|---|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | `Task.Run` + `CancellationToken` only cancels at checkpoints. If `work` is a single blocking call (e.g., `Thread.Sleep(60000)`), it won't stop until it finishes. Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`. |
| **Fire-and-forget work keeps running** | The first (minimal) migration returns after timeout, but the `Task.Run` thread keeps running in the background. Only the cooperative version actually stops work. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` leaks timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish your timeout from other cancellations. |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you can't modify `work` to accept a token, move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` — that's the only reliable way to forcibly stop uncooperative code in modern .NET. |

### The Fundamental Shift

**.NET Framework** — preemptive: the runtime injects `ThreadAbortException` at any point.
**.NET 8** — cooperative: code must **voluntarily** check `CancellationToken` and exit. No external force can tear down a thread. Design cancellation checkpoints based on your acceptable latency.
